<!--
  Safe PoC -> x8bitranjit.
  Benign PoC lab - x8bitranjit style. Authorized security testing only.

  WHAT THIS DEMONSTRATES: HTA (HTML Application) executes as a trusted local
  application on open - full script engine with WScript/ActiveX objects,
  NOT a browser sandbox.

  WHERE IT FIRES: double-click/open on Windows (mshta.exe). Delivery matters:
  if a platform (document center, attachment flow, support portal) allows
  .hta through to staff, "opening the attachment" = code execution prompt-free
  in most configurations.

  THIS FILE IS BENIGN: it only shows a message box and text - no WScript
  objects are created, nothing is executed, nothing leaves the machine.
  The REAL payload shape (authorized targets only) is the same file
  instantiating a shell object via ActiveX and calling its Run method -
  which is why .hta in an allowlist is itself the finding.
-->
<html>
<head>
  <title>Benign HTA PoC</title>
  <HTA:APPLICATION ID="poc" APPLICATIONNAME="BenignHTAPoC" BORDER="thin" SCROLL="no"/>
  <script language="VBScript">
    Sub OnLoad
      ' Benign: MsgBox only - the ActiveX/WScript line is intentionally absent.
      MsgBox "Benign HTA PoC - HTA opened as a local application (not sandboxed).", _
             vbInformation, "Benign HTA PoC"
    End Sub
  </script>
</head>
<body onload="OnLoad" style="font-family:Arial">
  <h2>Benign HTA PoC</h2>
  <p>If you see the message box, this file ran as a local HTML Application.</p>
  <p>A real payload would use WScript.Shell / ActiveX here - that is why
     .hta must never be accepted on document platforms.</p>
</body>
  <p style="font-size:12px;color:#666">Safe PoC -&gt; x8bitranjit</p>

</html>
